# Time Tracking & Invoicing Workflow

## Overview

Billable hours get logged per-client into the personal Obsidian vault, then compiled into an
invoice note when it's time to bill. This is a personal-machine-only workflow — there's no
work-machine equivalent, since invoicing reads directly from the vault.

## The `hours` Command

Quick capture from terminal, auto-detects the client from the current workspace:

```bash
cd ~/Code/<workspace>
hours 2.5 "Fixed CORS bug in tunnel config"
```

Appends a row to `~/Code/obsidian-vault-setup/02-Areas/Work-Ongoing/<workspace>/Hours.md`, which is one
`### YYYY-MM-DD` section and table **per day** (not one flat table for the whole client):

```markdown
### 2026-09-01

| Date       | Hours | Time        | Description                | Invoiced |
|------------|-------|-------------|-----------------------------|----------|
| 2026-09-01 | 2.5   |             | Fixed CORS bug in tunnel   | No       |
| 2026-09-01 | 3.00  | 19:00-22:00 | Client call, budget review | No       |
| **Total**  | **5.50** |  |  |  |
```

`hours <start> to <end> "<desc>"` (24h `HHMM` or `HH:MM`) fills in both Hours (computed
duration) and Time (the original range); `hours <amount> "<desc>"` fills in Hours only, leaving
Time blank. `hours --date yesterday|today|YYYY-MM-DD|MM-DD-YYYY ...` backdates an entry — it
can appear anywhere in the arguments — run once per entry to backfill several for the same day.

Keep descriptions very brief — a few words, not a paragraph. Detail belongs in the Daily
note's `## Standup` section (see `standup-notes.md`); this table is purely for billing.

Errors out on a work machine (`WORK_WORKSPACE` set) — there's no vault to write to there.

Each day's **Total** row is recomputed by the script every time it logs another entry for that
day — a plain sum, no target/goal comparison, so the user can glance at today's section and see
today's hours at a time. Day sections are kept newest-first, inserted by actual date (not by
when the command ran) so backdated `--date` entries land in the right place; re-logging to an
existing day rebuilds that day's section in place rather than moving it.

Right after the H1 header, the file carries a `dataviewjs` block that computes a *grand*
"Unbilled: Xh · $Y" line live, across every day — it's correct on every view, not just after
running `hours` (so a manual edit or a mobile QuickAdd capture doesn't go stale), and it's the
first thing visible on opening the note. The script keeps that block pinned there — right under
the header, above every day section — whenever it appends a row; don't reorder it by hand, and
if writing to `Hours.md` outside the script (e.g. fixing a row by hand), insert changes into the correct day's table, always *below* the
```` ```dataviewjs ```` block, never above it.

## Generating an Invoice

`bin/generate-invoice.py` in the vault does all of this for the client it's set up for — it
reads `Hours.md`,
assigns each row a category from its own `RULES` table, and writes the note. Run
`bin/generate-invoice.py --dry-run` to see the rollup without writing. It refuses to write a
partial invoice: any row it can't categorize is listed so you add a `RULES` entry and re-run,
which is the maintenance point as new kinds of work get logged. Generating never writes
`Hours.md`; closing a month (step 6) is a separate command.

Build it by hand only for a client the script doesn't cover. Either way:

1. Read `02-Areas/Work-Ongoing/<client>/Hours.md`.
2. Filter rows in the requested period where `Invoiced` = `No`.
3. Compute the line-item `Amount` for each row (Hours × `rate` from that file's frontmatter),
   and the Total Hours / Total Due. If `rate` isn't set, ask for one rather than guessing.
4. Compute `due_date` from the invoice date using `terms` (default **Net 15** unless the user
   says otherwise for that invoice) — e.g. date `2026-09-01` + Net 15 → `2026-09-16`.
5. Write a new note at `02-Areas/Work-Ongoing/<client>/Invoices/<invoice_number>.md`, following the shape
   in `03-Resources/Templates/invoice.md`. Sender block is fixed: Bennie Mosher /
   {{ if index . "invoice_address" }}{{ .invoice_address }}{{ else }}<set invoice_address in ~/.config/chezmoi/chezmoi.toml>{{ end }} /
   {{ .git_email }} / {{ if index . "invoice_phone" }}{{ .invoice_phone }}{{ else }}<set invoice_phone in ~/.config/chezmoi/chezmoi.toml>{{ end }}.
   Payment line reads "Direct deposit (arranged separately)" — never print bank/account
   details in the note.

   The note is three parts, each split by a hard page break so **Export to PDF** starts each
   on its own page (user's preference, 2026-09-25):
     - `.invoice-summary-page` — the first page (user's preference, 2026-10-01), a "Summary by Category" table with columns **Category, Hours, Rate,
       Amount** (in that order — matches the standard invoicing convention of title → hours →
       rate → amount), one row per category, ordered by hours descending, ending in a bold
       `Total` row that gives Total Hours and Total Due **in one line** rather than as a
       separate banner (Rate is blank on the Total row — summing a rate is meaningless). Every
       category used here must come from the canonical list below — see "Rules for AI
       Assistants" before inventing a new one. **Rate lives on the two summary pages, not on
       the detail pages.** The `**Payment:**` line lives on this page, directly below the
       Total row, so the reader sees it on page one without turning a page.
     - `.invoice-week-page` — the second page, a "Summary by Week" table with columns **Week,
       Hours, Rate, Amount**. One row per week that has logged time, the Week cell giving the
       week number over the actual date range worked (`Week 2` / `Sep 8 – Sep 13, 2026` — the
       days worked, not the calendar Mon–Sun bounds), weeks in date order, ending in the bold
       `Total` row carrying Total Hours and the **grand total** for the whole invoice. Weeks
       are Monday-anchored. Any flat line item with no hours behind it (see `EXTRAS` in the
       script) gets a row here too, with `—` for Hours and Rate, so the grand total is
       complete.
     - `.invoice-detail-page` — one per week, each starting a new page
       (`page-break-before:always;break-before:page;` on every block after the first) so a
       week never continues onto the page its predecessor ended on (user's preference,
       2026-09-25). Each opens with a week header rule — the week number, its date range, and
       that week's hours and amount — then one day-header bar + table per calendar day in that
       week (mirroring `Hours.md`'s per-day sections), and closes with a "Week N Total" bar
       that must match the week's row on the week summary page. Flat `EXTRAS` line items get their own
       trailing block on the same pattern.
       Each day's table has columns **Category, Description, Hours, Amount** — no Rate column
       (that's on the Summary page only, since it's a constant per invoice and repeating it on
       every row added no information) — and ends with a bold "Day Total" row; day totals must
       sum to the Summary page's Total row. Each day's header-bar + table pair is wrapped in
       one outer `<div style="page-break-inside:avoid;break-inside:avoid;">` — without it,
       Obsidian's PDF pagination can strand a day's header bar at the bottom of a page and push
       its table onto the next one. Wrap the pair, not just the table, so they always move
       together. The week's last day shares that wrapper with the "Week N Total" bar, so the
       total never lands alone on a page. A day with more than 8 rows is the exception: it may
       split across pages (its bar gets `break-after:avoid` and the column header repeats),
       since keeping it whole leaves most of the previous page blank. Detail tables use
       `table-layout:fixed` with shared column widths so every day's columns line up.

   **Styling is inline, not just the CSS snippet.** `.obsidian/snippets/invoice.css` exists and
   is enabled, but Obsidian's "Export to PDF" has been observed dropping it entirely (symptom:
   flex layouts collapse and adjacent `<span>`s render jammed together with no gap, e.g.
   "Terraform3.00 h"). `03-Resources/Templates/invoice.md` bakes the letterhead banner, table
   header colors, zebra-striping, and the Total row as `style="..."` attributes directly on
   each element for this reason — copy that pattern rather than reintroducing bare classed
   `<div>`/`<span>` layouts that only look right if the snippet happens to load. Color scheme
   is a single dark royal purple, `#3b1a6e` (user's preference, 2026-09-09: "purple and black is
   my Harley color" — a follow-up dropped the black variant, a second replaced the first,
   brighter `#6d28d9` with `#4c1d95` — "royal purple like Jesus would wear" — and on 2026-10-01
   that was still "a little bright to the eye", so it went darker and more metallic). The
   header banner and day-header bars add a diagonal sheen,
   `linear-gradient(135deg,#2c1254 0%,#4b2a85 50%,#2c1254 100%)`, over the solid color; table
   header accents and the summary Total rows use the solid color. Don't drift back to blue,
   black, or the brighter violets.
   The `**Period:**` line in `invoice-dates` is wrapped in an inline `<span style="white-space:
   nowrap;">` on the same line as itself, staying in the same paragraph as the Date/Due/Terms
   lines above it — this keeps the date range from wrapping without opening a `<div>` (a block
   element), which would start a new paragraph and add a visible blank-line gap before it.
6. **Only once the invoice has actually been sent**, close the month:
   `bin/generate-invoice.py --close YYYY-MM` (add `--dry-run` to preview). It marks that
   month's rows `Invoiced: Yes` and moves its day sections out of `Hours.md` into
   `<client>/Hours/YYYY-MM.md` (year first, so archives sort in order). Days from the next
   month already logged stay in `Hours.md`, so it holds only unbilled time and the
   `dataviewjs` banner stays accurate. It refuses to overwrite an existing archive, and a
   plain generate refuses a month with no unbilled rows, so a sent invoice can't be
   regenerated over by accident. Never close as part of generating a draft (user's correction,
   2026-09-25): a period stays open while there are still hours to log, and closing early hides
   unbilled work from the banner and from the next invoice.

   Closing keeps everything needed to revise a sent invoice (user's requirement, 2026-10-01 —
   a client may ask for changes). To revise: edit the rows in `Hours/YYYY-MM.md` (or the
   script's `RULES`), then run `bin/generate-invoice.py --reissue YYYY-MM`. It reads the
   archive instead of `Hours.md`, bills its rows even though they're marked `Yes`, and rewrites
   the same invoice number and date (taken from the existing full-month invoice note; pass
   `--date` if there's more than one). Git history keeps the version that was sent. A row
   backdated into a closed month lands in `Hours.md` as unbilled; move it into the archive and
   reissue, or bill it next month by hand.
   For a client the script doesn't cover, do the same by hand.
7. Tell the user to open the new note in Obsidian and use **Export to PDF** (Command
   Palette → "Export to PDF") to produce the sendable file — no plugin needed, it's a core
   Obsidian command.

## Summary Categories

Five categories, reused on every invoice so the rollup stays comparable period to period (user's
preference, 2026-09-30, replacing an earlier list of ~10 buckets plus per-ticket ones). The PR
or ticket detail belongs in each row's description, not in the category:

- **Meetings** — standups, syncs, 1:1s, team/project meetings.
- **Code Review** — reviewing, approving, or merging *someone else's* PR; also covers
  reviewing an infra/config PR (e.g. Terraform) even when the subject matter isn't code per se.
- **Engineering** — building things: feature and infrastructure work on a PR or ticket you
  own, testing, CI/CD and dev tooling, monitoring and alerting design, and documentation. A PR
  you take over and finish counts here, not under Code Review.
- **Admin** — paperwork, Slack triage, general overhead, account/machine setup, and
  travel/appointments (travel time is folded in here, per user preference 2026-09-09).
- **Software** — a pass-through tool or subscription cost billed at a flat amount with no hours
  behind it. Hours and Rate render as `—`; it belongs in the script's `EXTRAS`, not in
  `Hours.md`.

Don't add a sixth without asking. If one ever earns its place (Documentation is the likeliest
split from Engineering), add it here so later invoices reuse the same label.

## Rules for AI Assistants

- Never run `--close` (or mark a row `Invoiced: Yes` by hand) until the user confirms the
  invoice was sent — this changes billing state.
- Keep invoice notes idempotent-safe: check `Invoices/` for an existing draft covering the
  same period before creating a duplicate.
- If `rate` isn't set for a client, still generate the invoice with hours only and ask for a
  rate rather than guessing one.
- When a line item's category isn't obviously covered by the list above — it's genuinely
  ambiguous, or two activities are jammed into one logged row (e.g. "reviewed the Terraform PR
  and prepped the pre-commit PR") — ask the user which bucket it belongs in rather than
  guessing silently.
